    "description": [
        {
            "difficulty": "hard",
            "text": "The objective is to maximize the cumulative reward obtained over an episode in which a two-dimensional one-legged hopper moves forward as fast as possible while maintaining balance. The control is performed by a linear policy that computes torques applied to the three hinge joints based on the current observation at each timestep. The total reward per timestep is defined as healthy_reward plus forward_reward minus ctrl_cost, where healthy_reward is a fixed bonus of 1.0 awarded at each timestep the hopper remains healthy, forward_reward is the forward velocity of the torso weighted by forward_reward_weight (default 1.0), and ctrl_cost is the squared Euclidean norm of the action vector weighted by ctrl_cost_weight (default 0.001). The evaluation budget is {MAX_EVAL_VALUE}."
        },
        {
            "difficulty": "intermediate",
            "text": "The objective is to maximize the cumulative reward obtained over an episode in which a two-dimensional one-legged hopper moves forward as fast as possible while maintaining balance. The control is performed by a linear policy that computes torques applied to the three hinge joints based on the current observation at each timestep. The total reward per timestep is defined as healthy_reward plus forward_reward minus ctrl_cost, where healthy_reward is a fixed bonus of 1.0 awarded at each timestep the hopper remains healthy, forward_reward is the forward velocity of the torso weighted by forward_reward_weight (default 1.0), and ctrl_cost is the squared Euclidean norm of the action vector weighted by ctrl_cost_weight (default 0.001). The design variables consist of feedback gains and bias terms. The observation variables are: z-coordinate of the torso, in meters; angle of the torso relative to the vertical axis, in radians; angle of the thigh joint, in radians; angle of the leg joint, in radians; angle of the foot joint, in radians; velocity of the torso along the x-axis, in meters per second; velocity of the torso along the z-axis, in meters per second; angular velocity of the torso hinge, in radians per second; angular velocity of the thigh hinge joint, in radians per second; angular velocity of the leg hinge joint, in radians per second; and angular velocity of the foot hinge joint, in radians per second. The action variables are torque applied at the hinge joint connecting the torso and the thigh, in Newton-meters; torque applied at the hinge joint connecting the thigh and the leg, in Newton-meters; and torque applied at the hinge joint connecting the leg and the foot, in Newton-meters. The evaluation budget is {MAX_EVAL_VALUE}."
        },
        {
            "difficulty": "easy",
            "text": "The objective is to maximize the cumulative reward obtained over an episode in which a two-dimensional one-legged hopper moves forward as fast as possible while maintaining balance, simulated in MuJoCo via the Gymnasium interface (Hopper-v5). The control is performed by a linear policy that computes torques applied to the three hinge joints based on the current observation at each timestep. The episode terminates when the hopper becomes unhealthy or after a maximum of 1000 timesteps. The total reward per timestep is defined as healthy_reward plus forward_reward minus ctrl_cost, where healthy_reward is a fixed bonus of 1.0 awarded at each timestep the hopper remains healthy, forward_reward is the forward velocity of the torso weighted by forward_reward_weight (default 1.0), and ctrl_cost is the squared Euclidean norm of the action vector weighted by ctrl_cost_weight (default 0.001). The observation variables are: torso_height (z-coordinate of the torso, i.e., height of the hopper, in meters), torso_angle (angle of the torso relative to the vertical axis, in radians), thigh_joint_angle (angle of the thigh joint, in radians), leg_joint_angle (angle of the leg joint, in radians), foot_joint_angle (angle of the foot joint, in radians), torso_x_velocity (velocity of the torso along the x-axis, in meters per second), torso_z_velocity (velocity of the torso along the z-axis, in meters per second), torso_angular_velocity (angular velocity of the torso hinge, in radians per second), thigh_angular_velocity (angular velocity of the thigh hinge joint, in radians per second), leg_angular_velocity (angular velocity of the leg hinge joint, in radians per second), and foot_angular_velocity (angular velocity of the foot hinge joint, in radians per second). The action variables are torque_thigh (torque applied at the hinge joint connecting the torso and the thigh, in Newton-meters), torque_leg (torque applied at the hinge joint connecting the thigh and the leg, in Newton-meters), and torque_foot (torque applied at the hinge joint connecting the leg and the foot, in Newton-meters), each clipped to [-1.0, 1.0] by the simulator. The design variables consist of 36 continuous variables. Thirty-three of them are feedback gains that multiply each observation variable for each action dimension, and the remaining three are bias terms. The feedback gains are named as 'observation variable'_to_'action variable', and the bias terms are named as bias_to_torque_thigh, bias_to_torque_leg, and bias_to_torque_foot. Each design variable ranges over [-1000, 1000]. Please note that while these specified ranges define the absolute implementational boundaries for each parameter, they serve primarily as a reference frame; you can flexibly control the actual search space depending on your optimization strategy. The evaluation budget is {MAX_EVAL_VALUE}."
        }
    ],
